<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lucius Verus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">130–169</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucius Verus was Roman emperor from 161 to 169 CE, ruling jointly with his adoptive brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a>, the first time two emperors shared power as equals. He nominally led the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> of 161–166, whose returning armies spread the Antonine Plague. He died of illness in 169.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lucius-verus.jpg" alt="Lucius Verus" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucius_Verus_-_MET_-_L.2007.26_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lucius Verus was born Lucius Ceionius Commodus on 15 December 130, the first-born son of Avidia and Lucius Aelius Caesar, the first adopted son and heir of Emperor Hadrian. After his father died on 1 January 138, Hadrian chose Titus Aurelius Antoninus as his new heir and instructed him to adopt Lucius together with Marcus, Antoninus' nephew by marriage. Verus was educated by the grammaticus Marcus Cornelius Fronto and was reported to be an excellent student, fond of writing poetry and delivering speeches. He became quaestor in 153, a year before the legal age, and consul in 154.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a> died on 7 March 161, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> refused to take office unless Lucius received equal powers. The senate granted Lucius the imperium, the tribunician power and the title augustus. Dropping his name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commodus">Commodus</a>, he took Marcus's original cognomen, Verus. It was the first time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> had been ruled by two emperors. In spite of this nominal equality Marcus held more authority, and was alone Pontifex maximus. Verus' biographer wrote that he obeyed Marcus as a lieutenant obeys a proconsul. Together they went to the camp of the praetorian guard, where the troops acclaimed them as imperatores.</p><p>In late summer or early autumn 161 Vologases IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, then a Roman client state, and installed his own king, Pacorus. The governor of Cappadocia, Marcus Sedatius Severianus, led a legion into Armenia, was trapped at Elegeia by the Parthian general Chosrhoes and killed himself; his legion was massacred. Most of Verus' reign was occupied by his direction of the war that followed, which ended in a Roman victory with some territorial gains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to the Historia Augusta, Verus spent the years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> after the war keeping a troupe of actors and favourites, holding parties in a tavern built in his house and wandering the city unrecognised. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> disapproved but could do little, since Verus performed his official tasks efficiently. When the Marcomanni invaded across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, a war that lasted until 180, Verus fell severely ill on the return to Rome and died soon afterwards. Some authors suggest poison, which is unlikely; he may have died of the epidemic known as the Antonine Plague. Marcus grieved, and the senate declared Verus divine as Divus Verus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lucius Verus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hadrian made Antoninus his successor on condition that he adopt Marcus Aurelius and <b>Lucius Verus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-25">25 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>On the death of Antoninus Pius, Marcus Aurelius succeeded and made his adoptive brother <b>Lucius Verus</b> co-emperor.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-7">7 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At Ruwafa, Thamudic tribesmen built a temple in the 160s CE honouring the Roman emperors Marcus Aurelius and <b>Lucius Verus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/thamud">Thamud</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altinum">Altinum</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=155" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lucius Verus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=155">What was here in 155 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
